Here's the full rule to copy and paste:

xml
<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<!DOCTYPE Rule PUBLIC "sailpoint.dtd" "sailpoint.dtd">
<Rule language="beanshell" name="Remove_DEVU6_Entitlement">
  <Description>Removes the DEVU6NETSECTEST group (memberOf) from the R6-Core AD account of identity IVPT5898.</Description>
  <Source><![CDATA[
import java.util.*;
import sailpoint.api.IdentityService;
import sailpoint.api.Provisioner;
import sailpoint.object.*;
import sailpoint.object.ProvisioningPlan.AccountRequest;
import sailpoint.object.ProvisioningPlan.AttributeRequest;
import sailpoint.tools.Message;
import sailpoint.tools.Util;

String identityName = "IVPT5898";
String appName      = "R6-Core";
String attrName     = "memberOf";
String groupName    = "DEVU6NETSECTEST";

Identity identity = context.getObjectByName(Identity.class, identityName);
if (identity == null) {
    return "Identity not found: " + identityName;
}

Application app = context.getObjectByName(Application.class, appName);
if (app == null) {
    return "Application not found: " + appName;
}

IdentityService idService = new IdentityService(context);
List links = idService.getLinks(identity, app);
if (links == null || links.isEmpty()) {
    return identityName + " has no account on " + appName;
}
Link link = (Link) links.get(0);

// AD stores memberOf as full DN, find the real value
String groupValue = null;
List groups = Util.asList(link.getAttribute(attrName));
if (groups != null) {
    for (Object g : groups) {
        String s = String.valueOf(g);
        if (s.equalsIgnoreCase(groupName)
            || s.toUpperCase().startsWith("CN=" + groupName.toUpperCase() + ",")) {
            groupValue = s;
            break;
        }
    }
}
if (groupValue == null) {
    return groupName + " not found on account " + link.getNativeIdentity();
}
log.error("Removing " + groupValue + " from " + link.getNativeIdentity());

ProvisioningPlan plan = new ProvisioningPlan();
plan.setIdentity(identity);
plan.setSource("LCM");
plan.setComments("Removed " + groupName + " via rule");

AccountRequest acctReq = new AccountRequest();
acctReq.setOperation(AccountRequest.Operation.Modify);
acctReq.setApplication(appName);
acctReq.setNativeIdentity(link.getNativeIdentity());

AttributeRequest attrReq = new AttributeRequest();
attrReq.setName(attrName);
attrReq.setOperation(ProvisioningPlan.Operation.Remove);
attrReq.setValue(groupValue);
acctReq.add(attrReq);

plan.add(acctReq);

Provisioner provisioner = new Provisioner(context);
provisioner.execute(plan);

ProvisioningProject project = provisioner.getProject();
String msg = "Removal of " + groupName + " submitted for " + identityName;
if (project != null && project.getMessages() != null) {
    for (Object o : project.getMessages()) {
        Message m = (Message) o;
        if (m.isError()) {
            msg = "Error: " + m.getMessage();
            log.error(msg);
        }
    }
}
return msg;
  ]]></Source>
</Rule>
To use it, save the rule in the debug page and click Run Rule. Then check the Events tab and aggregate R6-Core to confirm the group was removed.

explaine it

The rule does its work in five steps.

1. Inputs (the 4 variables at the top)

java
String identityName = "IVPT5898";
String appName      = "R6-Core";
String attrName     = "memberOf";
String groupName    = "DEVU6NETSECTEST";
These say who the user is, which application to change, which attribute holds the groups, and which group to remove. To reuse the rule for another user or group, change only these lines.

2. Load the identity and the application

java
Identity identity = context.getObjectByName(Identity.class, identityName);
Application app = context.getObjectByName(Application.class, appName);
context is the SailPointContext, which is available in every rule. These lines fetch the Identity (Avni Ward) and the Application (R6-Core) from the IIQ database. If either one isn't found, the rule stops and returns a message saying so.

3. Find the user's account (Link) on R6-Core

java
IdentityService idService = new IdentityService(context);
List links = idService.getLinks(identity, app);
Link link = (Link) links.get(0);
In IIQ an application account is called a Link. This gets Avni's R6-Core account, the avnward account from your screenshot. link.getNativeIdentity() is the account's ID on the target system, which for AD is the DN CN=avnward,OU=GP1,....

4. Find the exact group value

java
List groups = Util.asList(link.getAttribute(attrName));
for (Object g : groups) { ... }
The UI shows the group as DEVU6NETSECTEST, but AD often stores it as a full DN such as CN=DEVU6NETSECTEST,OU=Groups,DC=.... If you ask to remove a value that doesn't exactly match what's stored, the removal quietly does nothing. So the loop reads every value in memberOf and keeps the one that matches the name, in either form. If none matches, the rule returns "not found" and stops.

5. Build the provisioning plan

java
ProvisioningPlan plan = new ProvisioningPlan();       // the whole request
AccountRequest acctReq = new AccountRequest();        // which account
acctReq.setOperation(AccountRequest.Operation.Modify);
AttributeRequest attrReq = new AttributeRequest();    // what to change
attrReq.setOperation(ProvisioningPlan.Operation.Remove);
A plan is nested in three levels:

ProvisioningPlan: the request for this identity
AccountRequest: Modify the avnward account on R6-Core
AttributeRequest: Remove this one value from memberOf
In plain words, the plan says "for Avni, on R6-Core account avnward, remove DEVU6NETSECTEST from memberOf."

6. Execute and check for errors

java
Provisioner provisioner = new Provisioner(context);
provisioner.execute(plan);
ProvisioningProject project = provisioner.getProject();
The Provisioner compiles the plan into a ProvisioningProject and sends it to the AD connector, which removes the user from the group in AD. The rule then checks the project for error messages. It returns either "Removal submitted" or the error text, and that result appears when you click Run Rule.

Why it's different from your two samples

Ranjith sample: A CertificationExclusion rule only decides which items appear in a certification. It can't remove access, so it doesn't fit this task.
NAVYA sample: This one had the right provisioning idea, but it was written as a method that never gets called. It also used ritm, which isn't defined, and it put the arguments in the wrong place. The new rule runs straight through from top to bottom.




